# evestack configuration — copy to .env.local (never commit the real file)

# --- Model provider -----------------------------------------------------------
# Direct provider call. No AI Gateway, no Vercel account, no markup.
#
# EVESTACK_PROVIDER picks the branch in agent/agent.ts: openai | anthropic |
# ollama. Unset means openai. A value that is none of those three is a hard
# error at boot rather than a silent fall back to OpenAI, because "ollamma" and
# "claude" are typos, not choices.
#
# EVESTACK_MODEL is optional everywhere and defaults per provider:
#   openai     gpt-5-mini
#   anthropic  claude-sonnet-5
#   ollama     qwen3
# Each provider reads its OWN key variable, so switching providers means
# changing EVESTACK_PROVIDER and the key — the model name alone does nothing.

# OpenAI — the default.
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-proj-...
# EVESTACK_MODEL=gpt-5

# Anthropic. Set the provider AND the key; ANTHROPIC_API_KEY on its own is read
# by nobody, because the openai branch is still the one selected.
# EVESTACK_PROVIDER=anthropic
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# EVESTACK_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5

# Local models: BOTH lines are required. EVESTACK_PROVIDER is what selects the
# local path — the model name alone goes to the OpenAI provider and the agent
# refuses to boot. Check free RAM first: qwen3 is 5.2 GB on top of Docker and
# Postgres, and a machine without headroom can hang. See docs/local-setup.
# EVESTACK_PROVIDER=ollama
# EVESTACK_MODEL=qwen3
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434   # bare host, no /api suffix

# Ollama only. eve sizes compaction from the model's context window and looks it
# up in the AI Gateway catalog; a local model is not in that catalog, so the
# template declares the window instead. 32768 is Qwen3's native size — raise it
# for a model with a larger one, and note that too high means compaction fires
# too late to save the turn. Ignored by the openai and anthropic branches, whose
# models the catalog does know.
# EVESTACK_CONTEXT_WINDOW=32768

# --- Durable sessions ---------------------------------------------------------
# Set by docker compose. Without it, eve falls back to its local on-disk world
# under .eve/.workflow-data.
WORKFLOW_POSTGRES_URL=postgres://evestack:evestack@localhost:5433/evestack

# world-postgres defaults to concurrency 50 against a pool of 10, which it warns
# about on every boot. Matching them silences the warning and stops workers from
# queueing on connections.
WORKFLOW_POSTGRES_MAX_POOL_SIZE=20
WORKFLOW_POSTGRES_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=20

# --- The agent's port -----------------------------------------------------------
# `npm run dev` AND `npm run start` both pass this to eve as `--port`, the
# dashboard container is pointed at it, and `evestack verify` reads it. One number,
# and everything that asks "where is the agent" gets the same answer.
#
# `start` used not to pass it, which meant a BUILT server took eve's own default
# of 3000 while verify and the dashboard kept looking here. 3000 is the default
# for Next, CRA and Vite, so on a developer machine it is usually already taken
# and the built agent served nothing at all.
#
# Set, because nothing else records it. `eve dev` defaults to 2000 and silently
# auto-increments when that is taken, so on a machine with two projects the
# dashboard — configured with a fixed URL — would drive whichever agent happened
# to hold 2000. That is the other project's agent, starting real runs, with
# nothing saying so.
EVESTACK_AGENT_PORT=2000

# --- Route auth ---------------------------------------------------------------
# eve fails closed. `create-evestack` generates these. `eve dev` skips them (the
# grant comes from the dev process, not the request); a built server requires
# them on every request, loopback included — see agent/channels/eve.ts.
EVESTACK_AUTH_USER=evestack
EVESTACK_AUTH_PASSWORD=change-me

# --- Dashboard ----------------------------------------------------------------
# When set, the agent exports OpenTelemetry traces to the evestack dashboard.
# This is the dashboard's own ingest route, not the conventional OTLP port 4318 —
# the dashboard is a Next.js app on 4000 and serves OTLP/HTTP JSON at this path.
#
# OPTIONAL. The dashboard's main views — sessions, turns, tokens, cost,
# approvals — read eve's Postgres tables and need no exporter at all. Traces add
# prompt bodies and tool arguments on top.
#
# Worth knowing before you turn it on: authoring instrumentation (which this
# does) stops eve installing its local tracing runtime, and that runtime owns
# both the `eve traces` spool AND the `agent.*` span vocabulary. What you export
# instead is the AI SDK's `gen_ai.*` vocabulary — same prompts and tool payloads
# under different keys, which the dashboard reads either way. The one thing with
# no exported counterpart is the root session id, so subagent traces cannot be
# stitched to their parent when exporting.
# EVESTACK_DASHBOARD_URL=http://localhost:4000/api/ingest/v1/traces

# The shared secret that ingest route expects, sent as `x-evestack-ingest-token`.
# It has to be the SAME value the dashboard has in its own EVESTACK_INGEST_TOKEN
# — the exporter proves itself with it, so a mismatch is a silent 401 on every
# span while the rest of the dashboard keeps working. `create-evestack` generates
# it; generate your own with `openssl rand -hex 32`.
#
# REQUIRED if you set EVESTACK_DASHBOARD_URL. Leaving it unset on both sides is
# not the "open" configuration it looks like: the route falls back to the
# dashboard's ordinary session auth, which an OTLP exporter cannot satisfy, so
# every span still 401s. Never to open, either — spans carry system prompts and
# tool arguments, and an unauthenticated POST would also let anyone write rows
# the dashboard renders as a real conversation.
#
# Why the mismatch is worth this much comment: @vercel/otel's exporter treats a
# 401 as a SUCCESSFUL export (the fetch promise resolves, so the batch is dropped
# and never retried), and it logs the status at debug level to a diag logger that
# is not installed unless OTEL_LOG_LEVEL is set. agent/instrumentation.ts probes
# the endpoint once at boot for exactly this reason and prints a loud line when
# the credential is refused — without that, a wrong token is indistinguishable
# from "no traces yet".
# EVESTACK_INGEST_TOKEN=

# --- Dashboard alerts ---------------------------------------------------------
# READ BY THE DASHBOARD, NOT BY THE AGENT — and they still belong in this file,
# which is the part worth knowing. The generated docker-compose.yml gives its
# dashboard service `env_file: .env.local`, so every name below reaches that
# container from here, exactly the way EVESTACK_INGEST_TOKEN above does. One
# file, both processes, nothing to copy across.
#
# /monitors computes its nine checks whether or not any of this is set. This is
# only what makes them LEAVE THE MACHINE. Unset, they are shown on the page and
# told to nobody — and the page says so, rather than letting nine checks under
# the word "firing" imply that somebody was notified.
#
# These were absent from this file entirely until 2026-08-19. That was a
# discoverability bug, not a broken feature: they have always worked from
# .env.local, and nothing a scaffolded user ever opens said they existed —
# packages/dashboard/.env.example lists them, and that file ships to
# contributors, not to you. Full reference — the transition table, why
# `firing -> not checked` is never reported as resolved, and the payload
# signing — is at /docs/alerts.

# The on switch. A Slack incoming webhook, a Discord webhook, or any HTTPS
# endpoint you control; the payload shape is picked from the hostname.
# Comma-separate for several. Unset, the delivery loop never starts at all.
# EVESTACK_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/T0/B0/xxxx

# Override the detected shape: slack | discord | webhook. Only needed when you
# post through a proxy that hides the vendor's hostname.
# EVESTACK_ALERT_WEBHOOK_FORMAT=slack

# Shared secret for the generic `webhook` shape. Every POST is then signed
# HMAC-SHA256 over "<timestamp>.<body>", sent as x-evestack-alert-signature
# beside x-evestack-alert-timestamp. Slack and Discord authenticate by secret
# URL and ignore this.
# EVESTACK_ALERT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# How often to evaluate. Default 60, floor 15 — below that the loop would
# evaluate faster than the checks it runs.
# EVESTACK_ALERT_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60

# Repeat a still-firing alert every N minutes. Off by default, deliberately:
# only state CHANGES are sent, so a monitor that stays bad is mentioned once. An
# integration that re-sends every minute is muted within a day, and a muted
# channel is worth less than no channel — it has trained you to ignore the one
# the real alert will arrive in.
# EVESTACK_ALERT_RENOTIFY_MINUTES=0

# Install-wide daily spend the "Spend today" monitor is judged against. Unset,
# it falls back to EVESTACK_BUDGET_DAILY_USD below (default 10) and SAYS SO in
# the alert text, because the two are not the same measure: the budget cap is
# per principal, this sums every priced turn on the whole install. On a
# single-user install they are the same number; with two users the install
# crosses $10 while neither person is near their own limit. Set it to `false` to
# switch spend alerting off without touching your budget caps.
# EVESTACK_ALERT_DAILY_SPEND_USD=

# --- Long-term memory -----------------------------------------------------------
# The `remember` / `recall` tools embed text into the same Postgres. Embeddings
# follow EVESTACK_PROVIDER, so you normally set nothing here:
#
#   openai     -> text-embedding-3-small, 1536 dimensions
#   ollama     -> nomic-embed-text,        768 dimensions
#   anthropic  -> no embeddings endpoint exists; falls back to OpenAI if
#                 OPENAI_API_KEY is set, otherwise memory refuses to start and
#                 says so. Set EVESTACK_EMBED_PROVIDER=ollama to keep it local.
#
# ON OLLAMA THE EMBEDDING MODEL IS A SECOND PULL. The chat model is not an
# embedding model, so `ollama pull nomic-embed-text` (274 MB) is required before
# `remember` works. `npm run verify` checks for it by name.
#
# Changing the model changes the vector width, and the width is baked into the
# table at creation. Set both together and drop `evestack.memories` — old rows
# were embedded by the old model and cannot be compared against the new one.
# EVESTACK_EMBED_PROVIDER=ollama          # openai | ollama; defaults to EVESTACK_PROVIDER
# EVESTACK_EMBED_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# EVESTACK_EMBED_DIMENSIONS=768

# --- Spend caps ---------------------------------------------------------------
# On by default at $2 per session and $10 per principal per day. An opt-in
# budget is a budget nobody turns on, and eve's own ceiling is 40M input tokens
# per session — roughly $120-600 — with output uncapped.
#
# This PAUSES at the cap plus at most one step already in flight; it is not a
# hard limit. eve threads no abortSignal into model calls, so a generation
# already streaming cannot be killed. See packages/evestack-budget/README.md.
#
# AND IT ONLY APPLIES TO A MODEL WITH A KNOWN PRICE. Spend is computed from a
# built-in price table, so a model that is not in it costs $0.00 per step and the
# cap above can never trip — the hook says so once at boot and counts the steps in
# `evestack.budget_usage.unpriced_steps`, and that is the only signal you get.
#
# The likely way to land there is the recommended one: pinning a dated snapshot.
# `gpt-5-mini` is priced; `gpt-5-mini-2026-08-01` is not. Local models are fine —
# `ollama/*` is priced at zero deliberately, which is the truth about them.
#
# Either give it a price, or refuse to run unmetered:
# EVESTACK_PRICING='{"openai/gpt-5-mini-2026-08-01":{"input":0.25,"output":2}}'
# EVESTACK_BUDGET_UNPRICED=stop
# EVESTACK_BUDGET_SESSION_USD=2
# EVESTACK_BUDGET_DAILY_USD=10
# EVESTACK_BUDGET_DISABLED=1

# --- Integrations -------------------------------------------------------------
# One-click sign-in to 1,000+ tools. Get a key at app.composio.dev.
# COMPOSIO_API_KEY=ak_...

# --- Channels -----------------------------------------------------------------
# All three ship in agent/channels/ and cost nothing. Leave a channel's vars
# unset and it registers its route, logs one line at boot and stays idle — the
# agent still boots and the HTTP channel is unaffected. Every one of these needs
# a PUBLIC HTTPS URL: none of the three has a polling mode, so a laptop needs a
# tunnel (`cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:2000`). Full setup, scope by
# scope and click by click, is in docs/channels/.
#
# None of these routes sit behind EVESTACK_AUTH_* — that policy covers
# /eve/v1/session* only, and no chat provider can send Basic credentials. Each
# channel carries its own inbound verification instead, so do not put a reverse
# proxy with Basic auth in front of them.

# Telegram — the fastest one to finish: BotFather gives you a token in a minute.
# Register the webhook yourself; eve never calls setWebhook.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456789:AAH...
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET_TOKEN=      # you invent it (`openssl rand -hex 32`);
#                                     # it is this route's ONLY auth
# TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=my_bot        # optional, no @ — needed for group @mentions

# Slack — bot token plus signing secret. No Vercel account and no Connect
# connector: eve falls back to both env vars when `credentials` is omitted.
# The signing secret must be set BEFORE you save the Request URL, because Slack
# signs the url_verification challenge like any other request.
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-...
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# Discord — the public key is the only one that is genuinely required at runtime;
# eve verifies Discord's Ed25519 signature with it and 401s everything without it,
# including Discord's endpoint-verification PING.
# DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY=
# DISCORD_APPLICATION_ID=             # needed to register slash commands
# DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=                  # optional: typing, proactive posts, fallback
# DISCORD_ALLOWED_GUILD_IDS=          # optional allow-list; unset = any guild

# --- Heartbeat (proactive agent) -----------------------------------------------
# The agent wakes on its own, works through HEARTBEAT.md, and messages you ONLY
# when there is something worth interrupting for. Off unless
# EVESTACK_HEARTBEAT_CHANNEL is set — a schedule that starts spending money the
# moment you scaffold a project is not a pleasant surprise.
#
# Off also means nothing is written down. The channel is read when the schedule is
# registered, so with it unset the handler is a no-op: no row in
# evestack.schedule_runs, no `evestack` schema created on its account, and no
# lastFireAt for catch-up to replay from. It used to be checked inside the handler
# instead, which is after the tracked wrapper has already claimed the fire — one
# INSERT and one UPDATE every hour, forever, for a feature that was off, in a table
# nothing prunes.
#
# Setting the channel and not the target is not a half-on state, it is a broken
# one, and it now says so: the fire is recorded as failed with the reason, instead
# of warning to a log nobody reads and being written down as completed.
#
# The channel must be configured above, and the target is that channel's own
# address shape as JSON, because eve types the target per channel.
# EVESTACK_HEARTBEAT_CHANNEL=telegram          # telegram | slack | discord
# EVESTACK_HEARTBEAT_TARGET={"chatId":123456789}
# EVESTACK_HEARTBEAT_CRON=0 * * * *            # default: hourly
# EVESTACK_HEARTBEAT_FILE=./HEARTBEAT.md       # default: HEARTBEAT.md in cwd

# --- Sandbox and dev-loop knobs -----------------------------------------------
# The image each sandbox container runs. Unset uses the one the template pins.
# EVESTACK_SANDBOX_IMAGE=

# "deny-all" (default) or "allow-all". There is no middle setting: the Docker
# backend honours only these two, and a domain allow-list needs microsandbox().
# EVESTACK_SANDBOX_NETWORK=deny-all

# Where the `docker` binary is. eve spawns it BY NAME, with no shell, so it has
# to be on the agent process's PATH — and that is the thing that changes when a
# service manager starts the agent instead of your shell. launchd's default PATH
# is /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin, which contains neither Docker Desktop's
# /usr/local/bin/docker nor Homebrew's /opt/homebrew/bin/docker.
#
# The failure is partial and therefore expensive: the agent boots, serves, and
# answers model calls, while every bash tool call fails with
# DockerUnavailableError. Set this (or PATH in the unit) when running under
# systemd or launchd — see deploy/ and docs/operations.
# EVE_DOCKER_PATH=/usr/local/bin/docker

# Skip `npm run dev`'s preflight checks and start straight away.
# EVESTACK_SKIP_PREFLIGHT=1

# Turn the one-line channel boot notice into the full story.
# EVESTACK_VERBOSE=1

# The dashboard lists the sandbox containers eve is running by reading YOUR
# Docker daemon, and it is off by default: a process that can talk to the Docker
# socket can start a container with the host filesystem mounted, which makes it
# root-equivalent on this machine. That default is right, and it is also why the
# Sandboxes page says visibility is off while the agent is plainly building and
# committing a sandbox template on every boot. Both halves are working; nothing
# joins them up for you.
#
# Turning it on takes TWO changes, not one, and setting this alone gets you
# "Docker did not answer" instead: the dashboard runs in a container, so the
# socket has to be mounted into it as well. In docker-compose.yml, under the
# dashboard service:
#
#     volumes:
#       - /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro
#
# Nothing in the dashboard writes to it. The page lists containers and offers no
# way to stop or remove one.
# EVESTACK_DOCKER_SOCKET=/var/run/docker.sock

# Relay eve dev's stderr exactly as it arrives.
#
# `npm run dev` drops two blocks and nothing else: rolldown warning that
# @vercel/otel calls eval, which is about a vendored protobuf helper this project
# never runs, and the workflow SDK explaining that deploymentId "latest" needs a
# world with atomic deployments, which self-hosted Postgres is never going to be.
# Both fire on every boot, one of them twice per session, and neither is
# actionable. See BENIGN_DEV_NOISE in scripts/checks.mjs for how each was traced
# to its emitter. Set this while debugging the bundler.
# EVESTACK_RAW_LOGS=1
